  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 3NT. West probably has fewer than 6 spades as he did not preempt. Still, it cannot hurt to allow him to win at trick 1. West will continue spades to South's ♠A.\n\nSouth should next cash the ♣A and ♣K. When West discards, South must find a way to guard against a likely 5-3 spade break. The best chance is to exit a spade. If spades are 4-4, this play won't cost. The defense will win 2 spade tricks, but South will be able to give East a club, establishing the suit in the process. The defense will take only 4 tricks.\n\nIf West began with 5 spades, he must cash them all or South will be able to develop the clubs.\n\nAt least 2 clubs must be kept in the dummy until the last spade is played. West may be rectifying the count for a squeeze against his partner. If East holds the ♦Q and ♦J (or any 5 diamonds), he will later be squeezed in diamonds and clubs.\n\nWest exits in diamonds. South must win in dummy. Three rounds of hearts are cashed, ending in the North hand. East is squeezed on the third round of hearts.",
